= Social information processing =

'''Social information processing''' (SIP) is a term used for several distinct theories about how social information affects judgment, behavior, attitudes, and interpersonal relationships. The name does not refer to one unified model.

Three major traditions are especially important:

# '''organizational social information processing''' — Gerald Salancik and Jeffrey Pfeffer's account of how workplace attitudes and perceptions are shaped by social context and prior behavior;
# '''developmental social information processing''' — Nicki Crick and Kenneth Dodge's model of how children encode and interpret social cues, select goals, generate responses, and act in social situations;
# '''computer-mediated communication social information processing theory''' — Joseph Walther's account of how people form impressions and develop relationships through communication channels with reduced nonverbal cues.

These traditions share an emphasis on socially available information but differ in domain, mechanisms, variables, and predictions. They should not be treated as different stages of one process.

== Organizational social information processing ==

Salancik and Pfeffer introduced a social information processing approach to job attitudes and task design in 1978.<ref name="sp">[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10307892/ Salancik, G. R. and Pfeffer, J. (1978), “A Social Information Processing Approach to Job Attitudes and Task Design,” ''Administrative Science Quarterly'' 23(2), 224–253.]</ref>

Their account challenged theories that explained job attitudes primarily through stable individual needs or objective task characteristics.

Instead, people use social information to interpret:

* what aspects of a job are important;
* how coworkers evaluate the work;
* what prior behavior implies about their own attitudes;
* which standards should be used for judgment.

A worker's reported satisfaction can therefore depend partly on the surrounding social environment.

== Social context and work attitudes ==

The organizational model emphasizes that attitudes are constructed in context.

Social cues can affect:

* attention;
* interpretation;
* expectations;
* reported needs;
* evaluation of task characteristics.

For example, coworkers repeatedly describing a task as monotonous can influence how another employee notices and interprets the same task.

The theory does not imply unlimited social determination.

Material working conditions, pay, workload, skill, autonomy, and individual differences still matter.

== Prior behavior ==

Salancik and Pfeffer also emphasize the consequences of prior choices and behavior.

People can infer attitudes from their own earlier actions, especially when those actions appear voluntary.

This creates feedback:

<pre>
behavior
→ interpretation of own behavior
→ attitude
→ later behavior
</pre>

The process can reinforce commitment.

It should not be reduced to conscious rational calculation.

== Developmental social information processing ==

Crick and Dodge's 1994 reformulation became a major framework for studying children's social adjustment and aggressive behavior.<ref name="crick">[https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.115.1.74 Crick, N. R. and Dodge, K. A. (1994), “A review and reformulation of social information-processing mechanisms in children's social adjustment,” ''Psychological Bulletin'' 115(1), 74–101.]</ref>

The model organizes social responding into recurring processing components.

A common sequence is:

# encoding social cues;
# interpreting cues;
# clarifying or selecting goals;
# generating possible responses;
# evaluating and selecting a response;
# enacting behavior.

The stages are analytical.

Real processing can be rapid, recursive, parallel, and partly automatic.

== Encoding social cues ==

In a social encounter, not every cue is processed equally.

Relevant cues can include:

* facial expression;
* tone;
* action;
* context;
* past history.

Attention can be selective.

A child who overlooks benign contextual information can interpret an ambiguous event differently from one who notices it.

Encoding therefore precedes interpretation conceptually, even though the processes can interact.

== Interpretation ==

Interpretation assigns meaning to social cues.

An ambiguous event can be interpreted as:

* intentional;
* accidental;
* hostile;
* friendly;
* irrelevant.

One widely studied phenomenon is '''hostile attribution bias''': a tendency to interpret ambiguous social actions as hostile.

The association between such processing styles and aggressive or maladaptive behavior is well documented, but the relation is probabilistic and context-sensitive.

A hostile interpretation does not inevitably produce aggression.

== Goal clarification ==

People enter social situations with goals.

Possible goals include:

* avoiding harm;
* gaining status;
* preserving friendship;
* obtaining an object;
* following a rule.

Goal selection changes which responses are considered attractive.

The same cue can therefore produce different behavior under different goals.

== Response generation ==

An individual retrieves or constructs possible actions.

A narrow response repertoire can constrain behavior.

If only aggressive responses are readily available, peaceful alternatives cannot be selected even if they would work better.

Social learning can expand the response set.

== Response evaluation and selection ==

Candidate responses are evaluated according to expected outcomes and beliefs.

Factors can include:

* expected success;
* social consequences;
* self-efficacy;
* moral evaluation;
* emotional state.

A response can be selected even if it is objectively poor because the expected consequences are misestimated.

== Enactment ==

The selected response must be translated into actual behavior.

Performance can differ from intention because of:

* skill;
* inhibition;
* emotion;
* changing context;
* intervention by others.

The model therefore separates response decision from behavioral execution.

== Development and social adjustment ==

Social information-processing patterns develop through interaction among:

* prior experiences;
* relationships;
* temperament;
* learning;
* social environment.

Repeated hostile experiences can alter expectations and cue interpretation.

Behavior then changes later social experiences.

The model is therefore recursive rather than a one-way pipeline.

== Computer-mediated communication theory ==

Joseph Walther's Social Information Processing Theory addresses interpersonal relationships in computer-mediated communication (CMC).<ref name="walther">[https://doi.org/10.1177/009365092019001003 Walther, J. B. (1992), “Interpersonal Effects in Computer-Mediated Interaction: A Relational Perspective,” ''Communication Research'' 19(1), 52–90.]</ref>

Early CMC theories often predicted impersonal interaction because text channels lacked face-to-face nonverbal cues.

Walther proposed a different account.

People adapt to the cues a medium does provide and can form individuating impressions and relational communication over time.

== Reduced cues do not imply no social information ==

Text communication lacks many face-to-face signals such as:

* posture;
* facial expression;
* vocal tone;
* physical appearance.

But text still carries social information through:

* word choice;
* timing;
* punctuation;
* style;
* self-disclosure;
* message length;
* emoticons and later graphical cues.

Users adapt communicatively.

The important question is how much social information accumulates and how it is interpreted.

== Time and message accumulation ==

Walther emphasized the role of time.

A text-based interaction can transmit social information more slowly than face-to-face communication because fewer cues are available simultaneously.

Given enough exchanges, relationships can nevertheless develop.

Walther and Burgoon's experimental work found that computer-mediated groups became more relationally positive over repeated interaction and could approach face-to-face groups on several relational measures.<ref name="walther-burgoon">[https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-2958.1992.tb00295.x Walther, J. B. and Burgoon, J. K. (1992), “Relational Communication in Computer-Mediated Interaction,” ''Human Communication Research'' 19(1), 50–88.]</ref>

This is a boundary claim, not a prediction that all online communication becomes intimate.

== Channel affordances ==

Modern communication channels differ greatly from the text-only systems studied in the early 1990s.

Current platforms can include:

* profile photographs;
* video;
* voice;
* reactions;
* persistent history;
* asynchronous timing;
* public audiences.

SIP theory must therefore be applied relative to the actual affordances of a medium.

“Online communication” is not one channel.

== Hyperpersonal communication ==

Later work by Walther developed the '''hyperpersonal''' model, in which computer-mediated interaction can sometimes become more socially intense than comparable face-to-face interaction.

Mechanisms can include:

* selective self-presentation;
* idealized impressions;
* asynchronous message editing;
* feedback loops between expectations and responses.

Hyperpersonal outcomes are conditional, not inherent properties of online communication.

== Three theories compared ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Tradition !! Main question !! Typical unit
|-
| Salancik–Pfeffer || How does social context shape job attitudes and task perceptions? || Employee in organizational context
|-
| Crick–Dodge || How are social cues processed into goals and behavioral responses? || Child or individual in social encounter
|-
| Walther || How do people form impressions and relationships through CMC? || Communicators across a medium
|}

The shared label should not conceal these differences.

== General social cognition ==

Outside these named theories, researchers also use “social information processing” more generally for the cognitive processing of information about:

* people;
* intentions;
* norms;
* relationships;
* social situations.

This broader use overlaps with [[Cognition]], [[Perception]], [[Theory of mind]], and [[Behavior]].

When writing scientifically, authors should identify whether they mean the general descriptive phrase or one named SIP theory.

== Emotion ==

Emotion interacts with social information processing.

Threat, anger, anxiety, or shame can alter:

* attention;
* interpretation;
* response generation;
* action thresholds.

The Crick–Dodge framework was later expanded by researchers who emphasized emotion processes.

A purely cold cognitive sequence can therefore be incomplete.

== Culture ==

Social cues are culturally interpreted.

Eye contact, directness, silence, and emotional expression can carry different meanings across settings.

A model trained or validated in one cultural population should not automatically be generalized to another.

== Social information and algorithms ==

Online platforms increasingly mediate social information through:

* ranking;
* recommendation;
* filtering;
* automated moderation;
* generated content.

The social environment perceived by a user is partly constructed by algorithms.

This creates a new layer absent from the original organizational and developmental models.

== Artificial agents ==

An AI system can encode cues, classify sentiment, select goals, and produce social responses.

This can imitate some functional structure of developmental SIP models.

Likewise, an AI can adapt communication style to a text channel.

These behaviors do not establish subjective social understanding.

Performance should be evaluated at the level actually measured.

== Experimental methods ==

Different SIP traditions require different designs.

Organizational studies can manipulate social information about a task while holding task properties constant.

Developmental studies can use standardized ambiguous scenarios and measure cue interpretation, goals, response generation, and behavior.

CMC studies can manipulate channel affordances, time, message count, and partner information.

Using the wrong method can collapse distinct theories into a vague concept.

== Epistemic boundary ==

Social information processing is a shared term, not a single universal mechanism.

A valid claim should state:

* which SIP tradition is meant;
* what social information is available;
* what processing step or relational mechanism is measured;
* what outcome is predicted.

Theories can be compared, but their constructs should not be merged merely because they share a name.

